WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:10.630
RL: We know that mitochondria may indeed be endosymbiotes in the sense

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:17.110
that millions of years ago, these tiny bacteria were engulfed by larger cells

00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:21.260
and ultimately evolved to be co-dependent with our cells.

00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:25.650
We provide them with foodstuffs and protection from predation,

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:30.790
and in turn, they provide us with the majority of the ATP energy currency

00:00:30.790 --> 00:00:33.290
that our cells need to do work.

00:00:33.290 --> 00:00:37.240
What you may not realize, however, is that each mitochondrion

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.990
has its own DNA, its own genome.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:45.170
So what that means is that while we are accustomed to thinking of our cells

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:49.010
and their genomes in terms of what exists in the nucleus,

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:53.320
all of the genes that we think of as the ones we inherit from our mother

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:58.520
and from our father, we cannot forget that the mitochondria in every single

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.940
cell also carry their own DNA genomes.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.920
Now, where did those DNA genomes come from?

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.630
Well, another interesting fact is that we only

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:12.330
get mitochondria from our mothers.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.760
The mitochondria come with the egg, while the sperm

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:19.550
that we got from our fathers, in terms of fertilization,

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:22.600
do not contribute any mitochondria to what

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:28.710
will ultimately develop in embryogenesis into us, into an adult human being.

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:31.980
So this is, of course, a debt that we owe are mothers.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.530
They are the source of our mitochondria.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:39.180
But then if you want to think about what we inherit and are there

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:41.900
possible mutations that we might inherit,

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:44.940
we can't just think about what we inherit

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:48.940
in terms of the nuclear genomes-- what we inherit from our father

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:54.620
and from our mother-- we also must think about what mitochondria we inherited

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:56.180
from our mothers.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:58.820
It was fairly recently when we discovered

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:01.620
that there are a number of human diseases--

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:06.540
usually metabolic diseases-- that are tied to the inheritance of mutations

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.880
in the mitochondrial genome.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.720
Many of these diseases, as you might expect,

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:17.240
are related to lower than normal levels of ATP or defects, for example,

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:19.410
in the electron transport chain.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.450
Defects that really affect, if you will, the energy

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:28.280
metabolism that we all need so crucially for all of life's processes.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:33.020
So the question is, where did these defects come from in mitochondria,

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:38.170
and how can we understand the process of inheritance that occurs?

00:02:38.170 --> 00:02:41.970
Let's begin by thinking about a cell's mitochondria

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:47.330
and what the makeup of those mitochondria might look like.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:54.800
So if we have a cell from our bodies, we have a nucleus,

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.630
and then we may have multiple mitochondria in a cell.

00:02:59.630 --> 00:03:03.170
Usually we'll have anywhere from 10 to 20 mitochondria.

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:08.660
Now, when we think about this, we generally

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:13.310
think of the nuclei or a single nucleus in a single cell

00:03:13.310 --> 00:03:15.960
as having a particular genome.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:20.590
But what we don't usually think about is that the mitochondria in a cell

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:22.820
are in fact a population.

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.240
They are not all the same.

00:03:25.240 --> 00:03:28.080
And in particular, if you think about their genomes,

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.410
their genomes may not be all the same.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:37.810
So here we're shown a number of mitochondria,

00:03:37.810 --> 00:03:41.440
and we're distinguishing their genomes by color.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:45.600
So we have, let's say, four mitochondria that we're

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.540
designating as having one particular genome in purple.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:53.420
We have two in pink, and we have three in orange.

00:03:57.080 --> 00:04:02.730
Now let's suppose that there's a defect in the mitochondrial genomes,

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:10.670
and the defect is associated with these two mitochondria that are pink.

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:16.740
What this means is that the defects are associated with two mitochondria,

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:20.540
but we have another four, in fact, seven mitochondria that

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:23.490
are otherwise perfectly healthy.

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:26.560
And so when it comes to the state of that cell,

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.540
whether or not there's a negative impact of those mutations

00:04:30.540 --> 00:04:34.330
will depend on how many mitochondria are defective.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:37.640
Mitochondrial diseases are really quite devastating.

00:04:37.640 --> 00:04:40.560
If you think about it, it's not really surprising,

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.650
because given the role that mitochondria play in providing the majority of ATP

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:49.140
that we need, any disease that disrupts their ability

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:55.020
to produce ATP, to manufacture ATP can have significant metabolic impacts.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:59.160
So mitochondrial disease often involves, for example, muscle weakness

00:04:59.160 --> 00:05:03.520
and wasting, because muscles use a significant amount of ATP.

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.800
It also can involve cognitive defects, sort of degeneration of the brain,

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:12.800
because as one would expect, neurons or nerve cells in the brain

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:16.580
also utilize an enormous amount of ATP.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:19.470
How common are mitochondrial diseases?

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:23.430
They're really one of the more common kinds of genetic diseases.

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:27.600
For example, in the United States, where you'll see a frequency of roughly 1

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:33.430
in 5,000 to 10,000 births might have some kind of mitochondrial disease.

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:38.170
Because of the severity or the possible severity of mitochondrial disease,

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:42.630
you also frequently see lethality by age two.

00:05:42.630 --> 00:05:47.280
So that quite often if there's a severe expression of a mitochondrial disease,

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.470
you might have a stillborn kind of situation,

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:55.190
or you may also have a case where the child dies by age two.

00:05:55.190 --> 00:05:58.640
So clearly, this is a very severe kind of disease,

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:03.370
but it also reveals a remarkably different kind of inheritance

00:06:03.370 --> 00:06:04.950
than anything we've seen before.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:11.645
[MUSIC PLAYING]

